1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,970
In September 2026, a single batch of
Windows updates broke four things at once.

2
00:00:06,811 --> 00:00:07,911
Audio stopped working.

3
00:00:07,991 --> 00:00:11,341
Remote Desktop stopped working.

4
00:00:11,421 --> 00:00:14,801
Shared folders in virtual
machines stopped working.

5
00:00:15,401 --> 00:00:17,901
And some computers stopped
trusting the company network

6
00:00:17,981 --> 00:00:21,391
they belonged to, so people
couldn't log in at all.

7
00:00:22,089 --> 00:00:23,779
One batch. One Tuesday.

8
00:00:26,279 --> 00:00:29,789
And if you're thinking this is
just another bad Windows update...

9
00:00:30,727 --> 00:00:31,927
it isn't.

10
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:35,357
Because this has been happening
again and again and again.

11
00:00:36,098 --> 00:00:38,488
So what exactly is their QA doing?

12
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:41,427
How can a company this
enormous ship an operating

13
00:00:41,507 --> 00:00:44,357
system update and not
catch something obvious?

14
00:00:47,107 --> 00:00:49,117
Let's start with the smallest one.

15
00:00:49,913 --> 00:00:53,763
Microsoft shipped an update that
changed a few things about the taskbar.

16
00:00:54,453 --> 00:00:59,103
And shortly afterwards, it admitted there
was a problem with mouse cursor settings.

17
00:00:59,995 --> 00:01:04,145
For some people, a custom cursor
would revert to a large white arrow.

18
00:01:05,135 --> 00:01:07,015
The kind of arrow that
makes you check whether

19
00:01:07,095 --> 00:01:09,985
you've accidentally booted
a computer from 1998.

20
00:01:11,642 --> 00:01:14,512
Your carefully chosen cursor? Gone.

21
00:01:14,742 --> 00:01:17,532
And putting it back
apparently didn't work either.

22
00:01:18,262 --> 00:01:22,752
Now, you might be thinking: Every
piece of software has bugs. Absolutely.

23
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,260
But there is a difference
between: "We found a rare

24
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:28,810
fault under an unusual
hardware configuration."

25
00:01:30,147 --> 00:01:33,737
And: "After our update, your mouse
doesn't look like the mouse you chose."

26
00:01:34,637 --> 00:01:36,667
Because somebody has to install it.

27
00:01:37,077 --> 00:01:38,587
Somebody has to log in.

28
00:01:39,057 --> 00:01:40,847
Somebody has to move the mouse.

29
00:01:41,557 --> 00:01:43,267
And apparently that wasn't enough.

30
00:01:45,477 --> 00:01:50,267
Then there is the audio. Install
an update. Reboot. No sound.

31
00:01:51,039 --> 00:01:54,139
Some USB audio devices simply stop, and

32
00:01:54,219 --> 00:01:56,869
Windows reports that
the device cannot start.

33
00:01:57,684 --> 00:02:00,474
And audio is not exactly
an exotic feature.

34
00:02:01,324 --> 00:02:04,994
You would think one of the basic tests
for an operating system might be:

35
00:02:05,739 --> 00:02:07,929
can the computer still make a sound?

36
00:02:08,779 --> 00:02:11,689
Apparently that is more complicated
than it sounds. Literally.

37
00:02:14,718 --> 00:02:17,868
And at home, all this is
annoying. Your cursor looks silly.

38
00:02:18,598 --> 00:02:20,728
Your speakers are quiet.
But you will survive.

39
00:02:21,749 --> 00:02:24,969
But a server that stops trusting the
network it belongs to means nobody

40
00:02:25,049 --> 00:02:29,999
can log in, and somebody in the IT
department is having a very bad morning.

41
00:02:32,829 --> 00:02:35,959
And this is where it stops being
a collection of funny stories.

42
00:02:36,808 --> 00:02:39,698
Because we can look at
Microsoft's own update history.

43
00:02:40,568 --> 00:02:42,318
Microsoft publishes it.

44
00:02:42,828 --> 00:02:45,278
Every month they list
what their updates broke.

45
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:47,927
And if you line up a single year of that

46
00:02:48,007 --> 00:02:50,797
list, the same pattern
just keeps repeating.

47
00:02:51,724 --> 00:02:54,694
January broke audio devices and webcams.

48
00:02:55,444 --> 00:02:58,354
February made the cursor spin
so the machine looked frozen.

49
00:02:59,289 --> 00:03:00,869
March produced blue screens and

50
00:03:00,949 --> 00:03:03,439
installations stuck
part of the way through.

51
00:03:04,469 --> 00:03:07,719
April broke the face recognition
people use to log in.

52
00:03:08,368 --> 00:03:11,418
May left File Explorer not
responding to the mouse.

53
00:03:12,348 --> 00:03:14,998
And October broke connections
to your own computer.

54
00:03:15,954 --> 00:03:19,404
Not the internet. The address
that means "this machine".

55
00:03:20,919 --> 00:03:23,209
And two of them are genuinely remarkable.

56
00:03:24,151 --> 00:03:26,961
One update broke the
button that resets your PC.

57
00:03:27,791 --> 00:03:33,001
Another broke the recovery screen, where
USB mice and keyboards stopped responding.

58
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:35,117
Read that again.

59
00:03:36,014 --> 00:03:40,644
Twice in three months, an update broke the
tool you use to repair a broken computer.

60
00:03:43,254 --> 00:03:46,504
And one more detail, and it
might be the important one.

61
00:03:47,154 --> 00:03:51,064
That audio bug, the one where
Windows says the device cannot start?

62
00:03:51,885 --> 00:03:53,495
It happened in January.

63
00:03:53,625 --> 00:03:56,655
And then it happened
again twenty months later.

64
00:03:57,466 --> 00:04:00,616
Same symptom. Same kind
of device. Same message.

65
00:04:01,487 --> 00:04:04,157
That is not a story
about writing bad code.

66
00:04:05,027 --> 00:04:06,757
That is a story about testing.

67
00:04:09,427 --> 00:04:10,957
And none of this is new.

68
00:04:11,806 --> 00:04:16,616
There was an update in 2024 that stopped
Linux from starting on dual-boot machines.

69
00:04:17,746 --> 00:04:20,256
The error message said, word for word:

70
00:04:20,971 --> 00:04:23,201
"Something has gone seriously wrong."

71
00:04:24,011 --> 00:04:28,501
Which is, honestly, the most accurate
error message Windows has ever shown.

72
00:04:29,248 --> 00:04:31,768
And in 2018 there was a
version that had to be pulled

73
00:04:31,848 --> 00:04:35,718
completely, after people reported
that their files were gone.

74
00:04:38,718 --> 00:04:41,328
So at this point you
might reasonably conclude:

75
00:04:41,678 --> 00:04:44,408
Microsoft just isn't very
good at writing software.

76
00:04:45,321 --> 00:04:49,351
And that would explain everything.
The bugs. The regressions.

77
00:04:50,041 --> 00:04:52,571
The updates that fix one
thing and break another.

78
00:04:53,271 --> 00:04:55,741
But there is a problem
with that explanation.

79
00:04:56,571 --> 00:04:58,741
Microsoft doesn't only make Windows.

80
00:04:59,684 --> 00:05:03,344
They make Office. Visual
Studio. Exchange. Teams.

81
00:05:03,424 --> 00:05:05,014
And a very large cloud.

82
00:05:05,947 --> 00:05:08,327
And all of those have
had their own bugs, their

83
00:05:08,407 --> 00:05:11,397
own regressions and their
own spectacular outages.

84
00:05:12,187 --> 00:05:16,047
And that matters. Because the
usual explanation for Windows

85
00:05:16,127 --> 00:05:18,957
is that it carries
thirty years of old code.

86
00:05:19,837 --> 00:05:23,347
A modern cloud service doesn't
carry thirty years of anything.

87
00:05:24,197 --> 00:05:27,787
It was built recently, by people
who knew what they were building.

88
00:05:28,575 --> 00:05:29,775
And it still breaks.

89
00:05:31,785 --> 00:05:34,135
So the problem was never simply old code.

90
00:05:35,065 --> 00:05:37,755
The problem is complexity
at Microsoft's scale.

91
00:05:38,603 --> 00:05:42,413
And Windows is the place where
that complexity is easiest to see.

92
00:05:43,137 --> 00:05:46,267
Because Windows isn't
really one operating system.

93
00:05:47,117 --> 00:05:50,287
At the bottom is an architecture
that goes back to the early nineties.

94
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:53,260
Then decades of interfaces.

95
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:57,970
Then compatibility layers.
Then drivers. Then services.

96
00:05:58,951 --> 00:06:01,661
Then newer frameworks, built
on top of older frameworks.

97
00:06:02,131 --> 00:06:03,451
Then a new look.

98
00:06:03,531 --> 00:06:05,361
Then another new look.

99
00:06:06,069 --> 00:06:08,409
It's less an operating system and more an

100
00:06:08,489 --> 00:06:11,179
archaeological dig that
somehow still runs.

101
00:06:12,053 --> 00:06:14,153
And Microsoft isn't
allowed to clear the site,

102
00:06:14,233 --> 00:06:17,243
because businesses depend
on what's underneath.

103
00:06:18,133 --> 00:06:20,203
There's a whole video about that one.

104
00:06:20,898 --> 00:06:23,108
What matters here is what it does to bugs.

105
00:06:24,078 --> 00:06:27,128
Every layer has to keep working
with every layer below it.

106
00:06:27,844 --> 00:06:31,574
So a change up here can break something
nobody has looked at in years.

107
00:06:34,254 --> 00:06:38,724
And there is one more clue about how this
goes, and it isn't about bugs at all.

108
00:06:39,449 --> 00:06:43,879
It's a pattern. Microsoft removes
something. Users complain.

109
00:06:44,909 --> 00:06:47,279
Microsoft takes years to reconsider it.

110
00:06:47,975 --> 00:06:51,345
And in the meantime, somebody
else builds a tool to put it back.

111
00:06:52,146 --> 00:06:55,816
And the really funny part is that
these aren't obscure hobby projects.

112
00:06:56,722 --> 00:07:01,732
There are entire applications whose whole
job is to make Windows behave more like...

113
00:07:02,471 --> 00:07:03,681
well... Windows.

114
00:07:04,691 --> 00:07:06,761
They bring back an older Start menu.

115
00:07:07,491 --> 00:07:09,001
They restore the taskbar.

116
00:07:09,511 --> 00:07:11,301
They change how Explorer behaves.

117
00:07:12,279 --> 00:07:16,109
And some of them give you more control
over Windows than Windows does.

118
00:07:16,876 --> 00:07:20,096
Microsoft has an enormous number of
engineers working on this system.

119
00:07:20,176 --> 00:07:24,326
And then somebody outside
the company says: fine.

120
00:07:24,636 --> 00:07:27,406
I'll put the old taskbar back myself.

121
00:07:30,166 --> 00:07:32,356
And now something else has changed.

122
00:07:33,066 --> 00:07:37,156
Microsoft is increasingly using AI
in how its software gets written.

123
00:07:37,901 --> 00:07:41,341
Its own chief executive has said
that twenty to thirty per cent

124
00:07:41,421 --> 00:07:45,371
of the code in their repositories
is now written by software.

125
00:07:46,317 --> 00:07:51,207
And that should mean more productivity.
More fixes. Faster development.

126
00:07:52,154 --> 00:07:54,144
But there is an obvious bottleneck.

127
00:07:54,874 --> 00:07:56,704
Writing code can become faster.

128
00:07:57,692 --> 00:08:01,942
Testing an enormous legacy system
does not necessarily become faster.

129
00:08:02,823 --> 00:08:05,203
And if you can produce
changes faster than you can

130
00:08:05,283 --> 00:08:08,553
test how they interact with
decades of existing code...

131
00:08:09,383 --> 00:08:12,153
you may simply end up
producing regressions faster.

132
00:08:13,148 --> 00:08:14,998
And it's worth being careful here.

133
00:08:15,808 --> 00:08:19,498
That figure is Microsoft as a
whole, not Windows in particular.

134
00:08:20,468 --> 00:08:23,478
And nobody has shown that
AI caused any of these bugs.

135
00:08:24,363 --> 00:08:27,513
Maybe AI didn't make
Microsoft worse at testing.

136
00:08:28,363 --> 00:08:31,033
Maybe it just made the
existing problem faster.

137
00:08:34,253 --> 00:08:38,363
By the way, if you'd like to see where the
rest of this goes, consider subscribing.

138
00:08:39,253 --> 00:08:40,683
It really helps the channel.

139
00:08:41,433 --> 00:08:46,023
So most of this video has argued that
Microsoft has a real problem with quality.

140
00:08:46,973 --> 00:08:48,363
And that argument holds.

141
00:08:49,233 --> 00:08:51,643
So here's the part that surprises people.

142
00:08:52,393 --> 00:08:54,283
Why might the kernel still be different?

143
00:08:57,063 --> 00:09:00,713
Because almost everything
described so far is not the kernel.

144
00:09:01,489 --> 00:09:06,259
The cursor. The taskbar. The Start
menu. The settings. The recovery screen.

145
00:09:07,070 --> 00:09:08,560
All of that sits on top.

146
00:09:10,890 --> 00:09:12,660
The kernel is the part underneath.

147
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:16,720
It decides which program
runs on which processor core.

148
00:09:17,250 --> 00:09:20,560
It hands out memory. It
talks to the hardware.

149
00:09:21,509 --> 00:09:26,099
And if that part went seriously wrong,
the symptom isn't a silly-looking mouse.

150
00:09:26,917 --> 00:09:29,357
The symptom is a machine
that stops, corrupts

151
00:09:29,437 --> 00:09:32,027
your data, or quietly lets someone in.

152
00:09:32,731 --> 00:09:37,521
And the kernel has something the rest of
Windows doesn't have. Hard constraints.

153
00:09:38,610 --> 00:09:40,830
A scheduler can't decide
to behave differently

154
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:43,920
because somebody
redesigned the Start menu.

155
00:09:44,807 --> 00:09:47,867
Memory management can't be
modernised by moving a button.

156
00:09:47,947 --> 00:09:52,697
And a driver doesn't get to assume
the hardware will behave nicely.

157
00:09:53,439 --> 00:09:55,399
Above all, the kernel
doesn't get to hide a

158
00:09:55,479 --> 00:09:58,489
mistake behind a slightly
confusing settings page.

159
00:09:59,359 --> 00:10:01,699
Which is one reason to
suspect it's a very different

160
00:10:01,779 --> 00:10:04,389
engineering story from
the rest of Windows.

161
00:10:05,257 --> 00:10:07,027
But suspecting isn't evidence.

162
00:10:07,937 --> 00:10:10,607
So look at what this thing
has actually had to survive.

163
00:10:11,585 --> 00:10:15,955
That architecture at the bottom
is old. Very old. And it survived.

164
00:10:16,931 --> 00:10:21,061
It became Windows 2000.
Then XP. Then Vista.

165
00:10:21,791 --> 00:10:23,741
Then 7, 8, 10 and 11.

166
00:10:24,561 --> 00:10:28,301
Processors changed. The
number of cores exploded.

167
00:10:28,381 --> 00:10:30,931
Memory sizes exploded.

168
00:10:31,221 --> 00:10:34,651
Virtual machines went from
exotic to completely normal.

169
00:10:35,437 --> 00:10:39,647
And the same family of code ended up
running servers all over the world.

170
00:10:40,397 --> 00:10:43,817
File servers. Company
logins. Virtual machines.

171
00:10:43,897 --> 00:10:47,767
Workloads nobody imagined
when it was designed.

172
00:10:48,667 --> 00:10:50,847
If the part that decides
what runs when, and

173
00:10:50,927 --> 00:10:53,257
hands out the memory,
were hopelessly broken...

174
00:10:54,247 --> 00:10:56,377
that bill would be impossible to hide.

175
00:10:57,156 --> 00:10:58,966
Now, an honest admission.

176
00:10:59,536 --> 00:11:02,596
Nobody outside Microsoft
can read the source code of

177
00:11:02,676 --> 00:11:05,546
the modern kernel, so nobody
can call it beautiful.

178
00:11:06,598 --> 00:11:09,148
Although somebody did
look at an older one.

179
00:11:09,851 --> 00:11:13,721
Part of the Windows 2000
source leaked in 2004,

180
00:11:13,811 --> 00:11:16,421
and last year a researcher
went through it.

181
00:11:17,094 --> 00:11:18,904
The findings were not flattering.

182
00:11:19,694 --> 00:11:23,684
Bugs that sat in that code for sixteen
years before anyone patched them.

183
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:28,550
But that is the grandparent of today's
kernel, and only a fraction of it leaked.

184
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:32,170
It tells us about 2000, not about now.

185
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:37,250
And surviving isn't the
same as being excellent.

186
00:11:38,159 --> 00:11:41,029
So this isn't a claim that the
Windows kernel is brilliant.

187
00:11:41,579 --> 00:11:43,309
Nobody knows that.

188
00:11:44,081 --> 00:11:46,891
But we do have decades of
evidence that it is competent.

189
00:11:50,221 --> 00:11:53,331
And that's a much stranger
conclusion than "Windows bad".

190
00:11:54,114 --> 00:11:56,494
Because it means the
answer to "what is their QA

191
00:11:56,574 --> 00:12:00,124
doing" probably isn't "Microsoft
can't write software".

192
00:12:00,994 --> 00:12:03,534
It's that they've spent decades
building new things on old

193
00:12:03,614 --> 00:12:07,564
things, without ever being allowed
to throw the old things away.

194
00:12:08,301 --> 00:12:12,151
The result is enormous.
Powerful. Compatible.

195
00:12:12,281 --> 00:12:13,671
And occasionally ridiculous.

196
00:12:14,630 --> 00:12:18,000
You install an update.
Your sound disappears.

197
00:12:18,850 --> 00:12:21,020
Your cursor turns into Windows 98.

198
00:12:22,005 --> 00:12:26,435
And underneath all of it, something is
quietly deciding which program runs next.

199
00:12:27,245 --> 00:12:30,195
Handing out memory.
Talking to the hardware.

200
00:12:31,150 --> 00:12:33,480
Doing exactly what it's supposed to do.

201
00:12:34,254 --> 00:12:35,804
At least... we think it is.

202
00:12:37,614 --> 00:12:39,364
Because unlike the taskbar...

203
00:12:39,534 --> 00:12:41,384
we can't actually see it.
